"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][12]={"university_physics_en:M12-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M12-L1 – Hilbert Space, State Vectors and Measurement<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Advanced Quantum Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Complex numbers, linear algebra,\neigenvectors, and spin from M8 and M11. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Use the Hilbert-space\ndescription of pure states, expectation values, and the Born rule\ncorrectly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>States Are Not Measurement Results<\/h2>\n<p>A pure quantum state is represented by a normalized vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a complex Hilbert space. Global phase has no measurable consequence:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{i\\phi}|\\psi\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive the same predictions. Relative phase between components, however,\naffects interference and is observable indirectly. Quantities are\nrepresented by self-adjoint operators. A superposition of eigenstates\ndoes not mean that one measurement produces several eigenvalues\nsimultaneously. The formalism predicts probabilities for an\noperationally defined measurement; state preparation, detectors and\nexperimental limitations remain essential.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Bra–Ket Notation and Spectral Decomposition<\/h2>\n<p>A ket\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a corresponding bra\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\psi|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe inner product\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\phi|\\psi\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be complex, whereas a normalized state satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\psi|\\psi\\rangle=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a discrete observable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith eigenstates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|a_n\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A|a_n\\rangle=a_n|a_n\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a nondegenerate eigenvalue\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Born rule reads<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(a_n)=|\\langle a_n|\\psi\\rangle|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>More generally use the projector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonto the whole eigenspace:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(a)=\\langle\\psi|P_a|\\psi\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe expectation value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle A\\rangle=\\langle\\psi|A|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_a aP(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a degenerate eigenspace a single arbitrarily selected eigenvector is\nnot enough to describe the outcome.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Measuring a Spin Superposition<\/h2>\n<p>Consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi\\rangle=\\sqrt{3}/2\\,|+\\rangle+(i/2)|-\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neigenbasis. Its norm is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3/4+1/4=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand an ideal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasurement gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(+)=3/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(-)=1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe expectation value is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle S_z\\rangle=(3/4)(\\hbar/2)+(1/4)(-\\hbar/2)=\\hbar/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Across many identically prepared systems the observed frequencies\napproach those predictions, while an individual result is always\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+\\hbar/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\hbar/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration decomposes a state into two orthogonal measurement\ncomponents; arrow lengths represent amplitude magnitudes, not\nprobabilities directly.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_132_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two spin-basis amplitudes √3/2 and 1/2, alongside probabilities of 75 and 25 percent\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two spin-basis amplitudes √3/2 and 1/2,\nalongside probabilities of 75 and 25 percent<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Amplitudes differ from squared probabilities.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Normalize\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\chi\\rangle=2|+\\rangle+i|-\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the probability of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|+\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter normalization.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why need an expectation value not be an allowed individual\nresult?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>5<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\chi_{\\mathrm n}\\rangle=(2|+\\rangle+i|-\\rangle)/\\sqrt{5}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(+)=4/5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The expectation value averages many outcomes and need not itself be\nan eigenvalue.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and Interpretation<\/h2>\n<p>The projection rule describes conditional repeat measurements only in\nan ideal projective-measurement model. Real detectors may require\ngeneralized POVM effects and open-system dynamics. A state amplitude\ndoes not assert that a particle carries a known classical value before\nmeasurement. A statistical mixture is represented by a density operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>≽<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho\\succeq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\rho=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than one ket. Its prediction is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(a)=\\mathrm{Tr}(\\rho P_a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCoherent superposition and statistical mixture can share one\nobservable’s distribution yet differ when another measurement basis is\nused.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Hilbert space, the Born rule and projectors connect quantum states to\nstatistically measurable outcomes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Time Evolution and the\nSchrödinger Equation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-73-introductory-quantum-mechanics-i-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">Introductory Quantum Mechanics I – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lectures 1–28, especially 5–15 and 19–25, lines 116–125; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L1 – Hilbert Space, State Vectors and Measurement · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M12-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M12-L2 – Time Evolution and the Schrödinger Equation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Advanced Quantum Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Differentiation, complex\nexponentials, eigenvalues, and M12-L1. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive unitary time evolution\nfrom the Hamiltonian and distinguish stationary from changing\nstates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamics of Probability Amplitudes<\/h2>\n<p>Following preparation, an isolated quantum state evolves\ncontinuously. This is not a classical point-particle trajectory but the\nchanging complex probability amplitude. The time-dependent Schrödinger\nequation reads<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i\\hbar\\,\\frac{d}{dt}|\\psi(t)\\rangle=H|\\psi(t)\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The self-adjoint Hamiltonian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines energy and dynamics. For a one-dimensional nonrelativistic\nparticle a common Hamiltonian is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=-\\hbar^2\\partial_x^2/(2m)+V(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe position representation satisfies the corresponding partial\ndifferential equation. Specifying a potential is a physical modelling\nstep rather than a mathematical formality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hamiltonian and Unitary Propagator<\/h2>\n<p>For time-independent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe evolution is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi(t)\\rangle=U(t)|\\psi(0)\\rangle,\\qquad U(t)=\\exp(-iHt/\\hbar)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U^\\dagger U=I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnormalization is preserved. An energy eigenstate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H|n\\rangle=E_n|n\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacquires only the phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-iE_nt/\\hbar}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso its probability density remains unchanged in isolation. A\nsuperposition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_n c_n|n\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngains relative phases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-i(E_n-E_m)t/\\hbar}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese can affect observables that fail to commute with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith time-dependent Hamiltonians, the evolution generally requires time\nordering, rather than a single naive matrix exponential.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Relative Phase of Two Levels<\/h2>\n<p>For levels\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1=\\hbar\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprepare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi(0)\\rangle=(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi(t)\\rangle=(|0\\rangle+e^{-i\\omega t}|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The energy-measurement probabilities stay at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach. Measurements in the alternative basis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>±<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\pm\\rangle=(|0\\rangle\\pm|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(+)=\\cos^2(\\omega t/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(-)=\\sin^2(\\omega t/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\pi/\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nan initially certain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>+<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutcome becomes a certain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>−<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutcome. The illustration shows rotating relative phase while\nenergy-basis populations remain fixed.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_208_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two energy levels with half population each and a rotating relative-phase arrow on a unit circle\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two energy levels with half population\neach and a rotating relative-phase arrow on a unit circle<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Constant energy populations, evolving relative phase.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(E_1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange for the above superposition?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(+)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfirst become zero?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does unitary evolution guarantee?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(E_1)=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega t=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\pi/\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Norms and inner products are preserved.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conserved Quantities and Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>For a time-independent observable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith no explicit time dependence, Schrödinger evolution implies the\nEhrenfest relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\langle A\\rangle/dt=(i/\\hbar)\\langle[H,A]\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncommutes with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits expectation value is conserved. This statement assumes suitable\nstates and operator domains; integrations by parts cannot discard\nnonvanishing boundary terms. A real subsystem that interacts with an\nenvironment generally does not undergo unitary evolution by itself.\nRelativistic quantum fields require a different formal framework.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Unitary dynamics changes relative phases and observables while\nconserving total probability.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Potential Steps, Tunnelling and\nScattering<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-73-introductory-quantum-mechanics-i-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">Introductory Quantum Mechanics I – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lectures 1–28, especially 5–15 and 19–25, lines 116–125; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L2 – Time Evolution and the Schrödinger Equation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M12-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M12-L3 – Potential Steps, Tunnelling and Scattering<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Advanced Quantum Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Schrödinger equation, complex\nexponentials and current conservation; M12-L2. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Match wave solutions at\ninterfaces and distinguish quantum transmission from classical barrier\ncrossing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A Quantum Particle Encounters a Barrier<\/h2>\n<p>Classically, a particle with energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E&lt;V_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncannot cross a potential barrier of height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn quantum mechanics the stationary wavefunction generally has a nonzero\namplitude inside the classically forbidden region; for finite barrier\nwidth this allows nonzero transmission. The particle need not ‘borrow\nenergy’ to cross. Tunnelling follows directly from the Schrödinger\nequation and boundary conditions. A forbidden region extending\nindefinitely in the propagation direction has no transmitted outgoing\nwave beyond it; finite width is essential.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Boundary Matching and Current Flux<\/h2>\n<p>For a rectangular barrier\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x)=V_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutside, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;E&lt;V_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe outside wavenumber is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=\\sqrt{2mE}/\\hbar<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the decay constant inside is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=\\sqrt{2m(V_0-E)}/\\hbar<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOutside one combines incident, reflected and transmitted travelling\nwaves; inside the solution contains both\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{\\kappa x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-\\kappa x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt finite potential steps both\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ψ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_x\\psi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare continuous. Probability current is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Im<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=\\frac{\\hbar}{m}\\operatorname{Im}(\\psi^*\\partial_x\\psi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Because both outer regions have equal potential,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=j_{\\mathrm{trans}}/j_{\\mathrm{in}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a rectangular barrier the exact expression is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinh<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=\\left[1+\\frac{V_0^2\\sinh^2(\\kappa a)}{4E(V_0-E)}\\right]^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Exponential Penetration<\/h2>\n<p>For an electron with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0-E=1.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\none obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>5.12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa\\simeq5.12\\,\\mathrm{nm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt barrier width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.50\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe attenuation factor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>5.96<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2\\kappa a}\\simeq5.96\\times10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis exponential factor is <strong>not<\/strong> itself the exact\ntransmission probability: the prefactor depends on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninvalidates the simple asymptotic approximation. A complete numerical\ntransmission needs both energy values and the matching conditions. The\nfigure displays a rectangular potential and schematic incident and\ndecaying amplitudes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_015_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Rectangular barrier above particle energy with incoming, decaying, and transmitted wave regions\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Rectangular barrier above particle energy\nwith incoming, decaying, and transmitted wave regions<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Amplitude decays within the forbidden region.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2\\kappa a}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthroughout the barrier generally invalid?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the attenuation factor already the exact\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The exponent doubles and the factor is squared.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It generally violates interface matching for a nonzero incident\nwave.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. The full transmission expression includes interface\nfactors.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Ideal Model to Devices<\/h2>\n<p>Electron tunnelling is central to scanning tunnelling microscopy and\nthin insulating junctions. Transmission can vary exponentially with\nseparation, making very small geometric changes detectable. Real\ntransport is often three-dimensional and depends on effective masses,\nelectronic band structure and interface states. The one-dimensional\nrectangular barrier is a teaching model rather than a universal formula\nfor tunnel devices. Absorbing potentials and time-dependent processes\ncan require a generalization of the simplest\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R+T=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncurrent balance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Quantum transmission through finite barriers follows directly from\nwave matching and probability-current conservation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Harmonic Oscillator and Ladder\nOperators<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-73-introductory-quantum-mechanics-i-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">Introductory Quantum Mechanics I – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lectures 1–28, especially 5–15 and 19–25, lines 116–125; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L3 – Potential Steps, Tunnelling and Scattering · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M12-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M12-L4 – Harmonic Oscillator and Ladder Operators<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Advanced Quantum Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Harmonic motion, commutators,\neigenvalues, and M12-L1–L2. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the spectrum\nalgebraically and explain zero-point energy and parity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A Local Model Near Equilibrium<\/h2>\n<p>Near a stable minimum, a smooth potential can often be expanded to\nquadratic order. The resulting local model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x)=m\\omega^2x^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nQuantum oscillators therefore apply far beyond a mechanical spring,\nincluding approximations to molecular normal modes, lattice motion and\nfield modes. Unlike a classical oscillator, the quantum system cannot\nhave precisely zero spread in both position and momentum. Even the\nground state has nonzero kinetic energy and spatial extent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Creation and Annihilation Algebra<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[x,p]=i\\hbar<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndefine<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>i<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=\\sqrt{\\frac{m\\omega}{2\\hbar}}\\,x+\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2m\\hbar\\omega}}p,\\qquad [a,a^\\dagger]=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The Hamiltonian becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=\\hbar\\omega(a^\\dagger a+1/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the number operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=a^\\dagger a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnonnegative expectation values and the ladder relations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[N,a]=-a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[N,a^\\dagger]=a^\\dagger<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyield integer eigenvalues\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=0,1,2,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor normalizable states and the spectrum<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_n=\\hbar\\omega\\left(n+\\frac12\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Ladder action is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a|n\\rangle=\\sqrt n\\,|n-1\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^\\dagger|n\\rangle=\\sqrt{n+1}\\,|n+1\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ground-state position density is Gaussian, excited wavefunctions\nhave\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnodes and parity alternates with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Zero-Point Energy and Transition<\/h2>\n<p>For a model vibration with frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>13<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=1.00\\times10^{13}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\pi f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe zero-point energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>0.0207<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=hf/2\\simeq0.0207\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAdjacent levels differ by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>0.0414<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=hf\\simeq0.0414\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nResonant electromagnetic excitation may drive transitions subject to the\ncoupling and selection rules. The figure compares the parabolic\npotential with horizontal energy levels and a Gaussian ground-state\ndensity near equilibrium. An energy-level line must not be mistaken for\na classical path in position space.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_200_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Parabolic potential with first four quantized levels and Gaussian ground-state density\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Parabolic potential with first four\nquantized levels and Gaussian ground-state density<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Parabolic potential and equal energy spacings.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_2/E_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Evaluate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a|0\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to level spacing when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5/2)/(1/2)=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The ground state is annihilated:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a|0\\rangle=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=hf<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Observability and Anharmonicity<\/h2>\n<p>Anharmonic potentials generally have unequal level spacings, so the\nexact harmonic ladder-operator algebra no longer applies. An ideal\nquadratic potential has infinitely many bound levels, whereas a\ndissociating real molecule has only finitely many bound vibrational\nstates. Although adjacent harmonic levels are separated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nspectral predictions also need the coupling operator: not every\nenergetically possible transition is optically allowed. Zero-point\nenergy is not a reservoir from which an isolated oscillator can freely\nextract usable work.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The equally spaced spectrum and zero-point energy follow from ladder\noperators of the harmonic Hamiltonian.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Orbital Angular Momentum, Spin,\nand Coupling<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-73-introductory-quantum-mechanics-i-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">Introductory Quantum Mechanics I – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lectures 1–28, especially 5–15 and 19–25, lines 116–125; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L4 – Harmonic Oscillator and Ladder Operators · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M12-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M12-L5 – Orbital Angular Momentum, Spin, and Coupling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Advanced Quantum Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Vector spaces, commutators and spin;\nM11-L5 and M12-L1. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain and apply\nangular-momentum quantum numbers and addition of two angular\nmomenta.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why Rotations Have Quantized Outcomes<\/h2>\n<p>Orbital angular momentum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑳<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒑<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf L=\\mathbf r\\times\\mathbf p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngenerates spatial rotations of a particle’s state. Intrinsic spin obeys\nthe same angular-momentum algebra but is not the classical rotation of a\ntiny extended object. In a central potential, energy and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be diagonalized together. With spin–orbit interaction, total angular\nmomentum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑳<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf J=\\mathbf L+\\mathbf S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes central. Components along distinct axes cannot all have sharp\nvalues, whereas total squared angular momentum and one chosen projection\ncan be sharp simultaneously.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operator Algebra and Angular-Momentum Addition<\/h2>\n<p>The operators satisfy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[J_x,J_y]=i\\hbar J_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand cyclic relations. States\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|jm\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobey<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J^2|jm\\rangle=j(j+1)\\hbar^2|jm\\rangle,\\qquad J_z|jm\\rangle=m\\hbar|jm\\rangle,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=-j,-j+1,\\ldots,j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen adding two independent angular momenta\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nallowed totals are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=|j_1-j_2|,|j_1-j_2|+1,\\ldots,j_1+j_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The product states\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|j_1m_1\\rangle|j_2m_2\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be transformed into coupled\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|jm\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstates through Clebsch–Gordan coefficients. Dimensions agree:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>j<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2j_1+1)(2j_2+1)=\\sum_j(2j+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Singlet and Triplet<\/h2>\n<p>Two\nspin-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsystems produce total quantum numbers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe triplet has three states:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>↑<\/mo><mo>↑<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|1,1\\rangle=|\\uparrow\\uparrow\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>↑<\/mo><mo>↓<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>↓<\/mo><mo>↑<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|1,0\\rangle=(|\\uparrow\\downarrow\\rangle+|\\downarrow\\uparrow\\rangle)/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>↓<\/mo><mo>↓<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|1,-1\\rangle=|\\downarrow\\downarrow\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe singlet is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>↑<\/mo><mo>↓<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>↓<\/mo><mo>↑<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|0,0\\rangle=(|\\uparrow\\downarrow\\rangle-|\\downarrow\\uparrow\\rangle)/\\sqrt 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Its total spin vanishes, even though each individual spin component\nis random and z-results are perfectly anticorrelated. The diagram\ndecomposes a four-dimensional product space into a three-dimensional\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a one-dimensional\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsubspace.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_270_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Coupling scheme of four product basis states into three triplet states and one singlet state\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Coupling scheme of four product basis\nstates into three triplet states and one singlet state<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Four product states become a triplet and a singlet.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalues arise for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_1=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_2=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Check the dimension count.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsinglet not a product of pure spin-z states?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=1/2,3/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+4=6=(2\\cdot1+1)(2\\cdot\\tfrac12+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is a coherent entangled antisymmetric combination.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Coupling Schemes and Selection Rules<\/h2>\n<p>Which angular momenta remain separately conserved depends on\nHamiltonian symmetries. In a strong external field a different coupling\nbasis may be more useful than for an isotropic isolated system. Optical\nselection rules do not follow from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone: parity, polarization and transition-operator structure also\nmatter. Two identical electrons must have an antisymmetric overall\nstate, so spatial and spin parts cannot be combined arbitrarily.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Angular momenta combine into discrete totals; singlet and triplet\nillustrate coupled-basis decomposition.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Time-Independent Perturbation\nTheory and Splitting<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-73-introductory-quantum-mechanics-i-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">Introductory Quantum Mechanics I – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lectures 1–28, especially 5–15 and 19–25, lines 116–125; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L5 – Orbital Angular Momentum, Spin, and Coupling · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-06-quantum-physics-iii-spring-2016/pages/readings/\">Quantum Physics III – Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 131–168; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L5 – Orbital Angular Momentum, Spin, and Coupling · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M12-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M12-L6 – Time-Independent Perturbation Theory and Splitting<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Advanced Quantum Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Eigenstates, expectation values,\nmatrix elements and angular momentum coupling; M12-L1 and M12-L5. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate first- and\nsecond-order energy shifts and correctly handle degeneracy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Solvable Model to Small Correction<\/h2>\n<p>Many quantum Hamiltonians cannot be diagonalized in closed form. If a\nsolvable reference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis available, an additional interaction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be organized using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=H_0+\\lambda V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe formal parameter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntracks approximation order; physical smallness must be judged relative\nto energy gaps and matrix elements. Perturbation theory is neither\narbitrary fitting nor a guarantee that all spectra change only slightly.\nClose to degenerate levels, even weak coupling can strongly rearrange\nthe eigenstates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nondegenerate and Degenerate Formulae<\/h2>\n<p>For a normalized nondegenerate reference state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|n^{(0)}\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nexpansion of energy and wavefunction gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_n^{(1)}=\\langle n^{(0)}|V|n^{(0)}\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Second-order energy correction for an isolated discrete level is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_n^{(2)}=\\sum_{m\\ne n}\\frac{|\\langle m^{(0)}|V|n^{(0)}\\rangle|^2}{E_n^{(0)}-E_m^{(0)}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_n\\simeq E_n^{(0)}+\\lambda E_n^{(1)}+\\lambda^2 E_n^{(2)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor degenerate levels, a denominator vanishes and this formula cannot be\nused directly. One must first diagonalize the restriction of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithin the degenerate subspace to identify the appropriate\ncombinations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Coupled Two-State System<\/h2>\n<p>Let a degenerate two-level system have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0=E_0I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>v<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>v<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;v\\\\v&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith real\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExact diagonalization of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=H_0+\\lambda V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mo>±<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>±<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\pm=E_0\\pm\\lambda v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The initial degeneracy splits by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\lambda v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe eigenstates are symmetric and antisymmetric combinations of the\noriginal basis states. The figure shows one energy level separating into\ntwo branches as coupling increases. Applying the nondegenerate\nsecond-order formula here would divide by zero, immediately revealing\nthat its assumptions are violated.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_150_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two levels E plus and E minus diverging from E0 with increasing coupling parameter lambda\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two levels E plus and E minus diverging\nfrom E0 with increasing coupling parameter lambda<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A weak coupling separates an initially degenerate level.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.030<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{nn}=0.030\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfind the first-order shift.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the splitting for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.020\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must a degenerate subspace be diagonalized first?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0030<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda V_{nn}=0.0030\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.040<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\lambda v=0.040\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The nondegenerate formula would contain zero energy\ndenominators.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When the Approximation Breaks Down<\/h2>\n<p>Nondegenerate perturbation theory requires coupling to other states\nto be small compared with relevant energy separations. Continua and\nresonances may demand scattering methods or regularization. Even with a\nsmall parameter, high-order series can be asymptotic rather than\nconvergent. In practice, estimate the next correction or compare the\nresult with numerical diagonalization of a tractable truncated matrix.\nThe sign of a second-order shift is not universally positive: the energy\ndenominator matters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Perturbation theory systematically treats weak interactions;\ndegeneracies require the right basis first.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Variational Principle and Ritz\nMethod<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-73-introductory-quantum-mechanics-i-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">Introductory Quantum Mechanics I – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lectures 1–28, especially 5–15 and 19–25, lines 116–125; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L6 – Time-Independent Perturbation Theory and Splitting · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-06-quantum-physics-iii-spring-2016/pages/readings/\">Quantum Physics III – Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 131–168; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L6 – Time-Independent Perturbation Theory and Splitting · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M12-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M12-L7 – Variational Principle and Ritz Method<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Advanced Quantum Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Normalized states, expectation values\nand single-variable optimization; M12-L1–L2. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the ground-state upper\nbound and optimize a trial state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Estimating Without an Exact Wavefunction<\/h2>\n<p>For a complicated potential the exact ground-state wavefunction is\noften unknown. The variational principle nevertheless provides a\ncontrolled energy estimate using normalized admissible trial states.\nInstead of solving the full differential equation, one minimizes an\nexpectation value within a chosen family. A simple family may have just\none width parameter. The resulting estimate is not a lower bound and\ndoes not automatically measure wavefunction accuracy. The primary\nstatement concerns the lowest energy of a Hamiltonian bounded below.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Spectral Theorem and Upper Bound<\/h2>\n<p>Expand a normalized trial state as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\phi\\rangle=\\sum_n c_n|n\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin an eigenbasis of the Hamiltonian. Then<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\phi|H|\\phi\\rangle=\\sum_n |c_n|^2 E_n\\ge E_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This weighted mean cannot lie below the lowest eigenvalue. For\nunnormalized states use the Rayleigh quotient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E[\\phi]=\\langle\\phi|H|\\phi\\rangle/\\langle\\phi|\\phi\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMinimization over a finite linear subspace gives the Ritz eigenvalue\nproblem. Enlarging the allowed trial space cannot increase its minimum.\nThe argument requires a well-defined bounded-below Hamiltonian and trial\nfunctions in an appropriate quadratic-form domain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Gaussian Trial State for the Oscillator<\/h2>\n<p>For the one-dimensional oscillator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=p^2/(2m)+m\\omega^2x^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchoose the normalized Gaussian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_\\alpha(x)=(2\\alpha/\\pi)^{1/4}e^{-\\alpha x^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne finds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle x^2\\rangle=1/(4\\alpha)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle p^2\\rangle=\\hbar^2\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E(\\alpha)=\\frac{\\hbar^2\\alpha}{2m}+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{8\\alpha}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Solving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\mathcal E/d\\alpha=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=m\\omega/(2\\hbar)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E_{\\min}=\\hbar\\omega/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis ansatz happens to reproduce the exact ground state because its true\nwavefunction is Gaussian. The figure plots energy against trial width\nand marks the minimum.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_309_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Curve E(alpha)=alpha/2+1/(8alpha), minimum alpha one half and energy one half\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Curve E(alpha)=alpha/2+1/(8alpha),\nminimum alpha one half and energy one half<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The minimum bounds ground-state energy from above.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can a normalized trial energy lie below\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E(\\alpha)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha\\to0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give the minimizing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=\\omega=\\hbar=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No, provided the state is admissible and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwell defined.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Energy diverges in both limits.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E_{\\min}=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Optimization and Methodological Pitfalls<\/h2>\n<p>Variational quality depends strongly on the trial family. Boundary\nconditions, symmetry, normalizability and singularities all matter. An\ninflexible family may give a valid but poor upper bound; excited-state\nestimates need orthogonality constraints relative to lower states.\nNumerical Ritz methods use many basis functions and solve generalized\neigenvalue problems\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒄<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒄<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\mathbf c=E S\\mathbf c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA small energy error does not guarantee equally small errors in all\nother observables.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The variational principle supplies controlled upper bounds on\nground-state energy within admissible trial spaces.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">WKB Approximation and Classical\nTurning Points<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-73-introductory-quantum-mechanics-i-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">Introductory Quantum Mechanics I – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lectures 1–28, especially 5–15 and 19–25, lines 116–125; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L7 – Variational Principle and Ritz Method · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M12-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M12-L8 – WKB Approximation and Classical Turning Points<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Advanced Quantum Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Stationary Schrödinger equation,\ndifferential equations, and tunnelling; M12-L3. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain semiclassical phases,\nexponential attenuation and the limits of WKB.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Between Classical Motion and Wave Description<\/h2>\n<p>The WKB approximation (Wentzel–Kramers–Brillouin) links the\nSchrödinger equation to a local classical momentum. If wavelength is\nshort compared with the variation scale of the potential, one may treat\namplitude as slowly varying and phase as rapidly varying. It describes\noscillations in classically allowed regions and exponential attenuation\nin forbidden ones. Their boundary is a classical turning point where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=V(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPrecisely at that point the standard local WKB expression ceases to be\ndirectly valid.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local Momentum and Validity Condition<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)=\\sqrt{2m(E-V(x))}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe leading WKB solution in an allowed region is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><msqrt><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/msup><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><msqrt><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/msup><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(x)\\approx\\frac{C_+}{\\sqrt{p(x)}}e^{(i/\\hbar)\\int^x p(u)du}+\\frac{C_-}{\\sqrt{p(x)}}e^{-(i/\\hbar)\\int^x p(u)du}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In forbidden regions define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa(x)=\\sqrt{2m(V(x)-E)}/\\hbar<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand use growing or decaying exponentials. A schematic criterion is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≪<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\hbar p&#39;(x)|\\ll p(x)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt a turning point with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe prefactor diverges; local Airy-function solutions and connection\nformulae are needed. For two simple turning points, leading\nBohr–Sommerfeld quantization reads\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{x_1}^{x_2}p(x)dx\\approx\\pi\\hbar(n+1/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Rectangular Barrier<\/h2>\n<p>For a high, broad barrier with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x)&gt;E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nleading tunnelling behaviour is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/msubsup><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\propto\\exp\\!\\left[-2\\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\\kappa(x)\\,dx\\right]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With a constant offset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0-E=1.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an electron,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>5.12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa\\simeq5.12\\,\\mathrm{nm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAcross\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1.0\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe exponential factor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10.25<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>3.5<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-10.25}\\simeq3.5\\times10^{-5}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe proportionality sign emphasizes that interface and reflection\nprefactors were not included. The figure shows a smooth potential mound\nwith two turning points and distinguishes oscillating from evanescent\nregions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_319_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Smooth potential barrier intersecting energy E at turning points x1 and x2, with attenuated wave region\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Smooth potential barrier intersecting\nenergy E at turning points x1 and x2, with attenuated wave\nregion<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Oscillation outside, attenuation between turning points.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How is a classical turning point identified?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the exponential factor change when constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must the local WKB wavefunction not be continued unchanged\nthrough a turning point?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">There\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=V(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2\\kappa a}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-4\\kappa a}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits square.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The local approximation formally diverges, so a regular local\nsolution and matching are required.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection Formulae, Not Naive Continuation<\/h2>\n<p>WKB becomes inaccurate when the potential varies too rapidly, the\nenergy lies close to the barrier top, or multiple reflections create\nstrong resonances. A rectangular step is abrupt, violating the\nslow-variation assumption; its WKB exponent is only a rough guide, while\nexact matching from M12-L3 is preferable. The quantization rule also\ndepends on turning-point structure and boundary conditions. A careful\ncalculation therefore states the potential, identifies semiclassical\nregions and specifies which connection formulae are used.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>WKB explains wave phases and tunnelling attenuation in semiclassical\nregions, but turning points require special matching.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Molecular Rotation and\nVibration<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-06-quantum-physics-iii-spring-2016/pages/readings/\">Quantum Physics III – Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 131–168; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L8 – WKB Approximation and Classical Turning Points · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M12-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M12-L9 – Molecular Rotation and Vibration<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Advanced Quantum Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Angular momentum, harmonic oscillator\nand photon energy; M12-L4–L5. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish rotational and\nvibrational levels and estimate simple molecular spectra\nquantitatively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diatomic Molecules as Quantum Systems<\/h2>\n<p>A diatomic molecule has internal motions that can approximately be\nseparated into rotation and vibration. Near the equilibrium bond length,\nharmonic motion is a useful model; orientation is described by a rigid\nrotor. These are distinct degrees of freedom that can couple in a real\nmolecule. Spectra therefore often consist of bands with rotational\nstructure rather than one isolated line. Separating electronic,\nvibrational and rotational energy scales is an approximation whose\naccuracy depends on molecule, excitation and temperature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rotor, Oscillator and Selection Rules<\/h2>\n<p>For atoms of masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_1,m_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe reduced mass is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=m_1m_2/(m_1+m_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe moment of inertia at bond length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><msubsup><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\mu r_e^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA linear rigid rotor has energies<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_J=\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2I}J(J+1),\\qquad J=0,1,2,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A harmonic vibration has<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_v=\\hbar\\omega(v+1/2),\\qquad\\omega=\\sqrt{k/\\mu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Electric-dipole pure rotational transitions of a polar molecule\ntypically obey\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta J=\\pm1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhomonuclear diatomics have no permanent electric dipole and lack\nordinary electric-dipole pure rotational lines. Infrared vibrational\nactivity requires the dipole moment to change as the bond length\nchanges.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Rotational Spacing and Isotope Effect<\/h2>\n<p>Take a model with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>46<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=1.00\\times10^{-46}\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransition has<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>I<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>1.11<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>22<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=\\frac{\\hbar^2}{I}\\simeq1.11\\times10^{-22}\\,\\mathrm J,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>corresponding to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>1.68<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>11<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=\\Delta E/h\\simeq1.68\\times10^{11}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf reduced mass doubles while geometry stays fixed, the rotational\ntransition frequency halves because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\propto\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nVibrational frequency decreases only by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure separates the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(J+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrotational ladder from unequal spacings of levels in an anharmonic\nmolecular potential.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_181_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Rotor energy levels J zero to three and a Morse-like potential with decreasing vibrational spacings\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Rotor energy levels J zero to three and a\nMorse-like potential with decreasing vibrational spacings<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Rotation: J(J+1); vibration: unequal spacings in a\npotential.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_2/E_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a rigid rotor.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if reduced mass quadruples at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a pure rotational line absent for a homonuclear diatomic in\nthe simplest electric-dipole model?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_2/E_1=6/2=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">There is no permanent electric dipole and the electric-dipole\nrotational transition moment vanishes.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Anharmonicity and Real Spectra<\/h2>\n<p>The rigid-rotor model neglects rotational bond stretching and the\nharmonic model has no dissociation threshold. A Morse potential gives an\ninstructive improvement, with finitely many bound vibrational levels.\nReal spectra can show rotation–vibration coupling, isotopic patterns,\nnuclear-spin statistics, Stark and Zeeman shifts, and Doppler or\ncollision broadening. Temperature chiefly changes populations and thus\nline intensities as well as indirectly affecting the environment; it is\nnot simply a universal rescaling of ideal energy gaps. Assigning an\nobserved line requires the transition operator and its selection\nrules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Rotation and vibration form distinct quantized energy scales;\nselection rules and molecular structure determine observed spectra.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-06-quantum-physics-iii-spring-2016/pages/readings/\">Quantum Physics III – Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 131–168; topic-level support, not a complete line-by-line review; Subject relevance to lesson: M12-L9 – Molecular Rotation and Vibration · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
